{{nihongo|'''Reijiro Masuko'''|増子 礼次郎|Masuko Reijirō}} was a Sanese naval officer and politician who served as the 9th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from 1913 to 1916. A Navy Admiral appointed as a "non-party" compromise prime minister to end the political crisis between the Army and the civilian government, Masuko's "national unity" cabinet successfully passed the military budgets and governed through a period of growing tension with [[Illypnian]] colonial powers in the [[Shendan]] region. His administration represented the military's growing influence over civilian politics and the difficulty of maintaining constitutional governance under the [[Saito Constitution]].

== Early life ==
Reijiro Masuko was born on 17 August 1852 in [[Fukushima Prefecture|Fukushima]], the son of a former samurai who had joined the fledgling Imperial Sanese Navy after the [[Saito Restoration]]. The Masuko family belonged to the new military aristocracy that had emerged from the Restoration, and young Reijiro was expected from birth to follow a military career.

Masuko entered the Naval Academy at Etajima in 1868, as one of its earliest cadets. He graduated near the top of his class in 1872 and was commissioned as a junior officer in the Imperial Sanese Navy. His early career was marked by rapid promotion, driven by the Navy's desperate need for trained officers during the period of military modernization.

In 1878, Masuko was sent to [[Moselle]] for two years of advanced naval training, where he studied fleet tactics and naval strategy. His time in Moselle left a lasting impression: he became a firm advocate of the "big navy" school of strategic thought, which held that Okaiken's security depended on command of the seas and the ability to project power into the [[Shendan]] region.

== Political career ==
Masuko rose through the ranks of the Imperial Sanese Navy with distinction, achieving the rank of Rear Admiral in 1895 and Vice Admiral in 1902. He commanded the Second Fleet during the naval operations in the Shendan in the late 1890s, where he developed a reputation for aggressive tactics and strategic boldness.

In 1905, Masuko was appointed to the Supreme War Council, the body that advised the Emperor on military strategy. His appointment brought him into direct contact with the political leadership, and he quickly became known as a vocal advocate for increased naval spending and a more assertive foreign policy in the Shendan.

Masuko's political views were those of a moderate military conservative. He believed in the supremacy of the military within its own sphere—particularly in matters of strategy and operations—but he also recognized the importance of civilian government for domestic administration. This made him an acceptable figure to both the military establishment and the civilian political leaders who were seeking a way out of the political crisis of 1913.

== Premiership (1913–1916) ==
Masuko was appointed Prime Minister on 17 February 1913, following the resignation of [[Ryuichi Koyanagi]] after the Army withdrew its officers from the cabinet in protest over the government's refusal to approve increased military spending. The political crisis had paralyzed the government for weeks, and the genrō council concluded that only a military figure could restore stability.

Masuko's appointment was a compromise: the political parties had demanded a civilian prime minister, while the Army insisted on a figure who would not challenge the military's autonomy. As a Navy Admiral rather than an Army general, Masuko was acceptable to the parties—Navy officers were seen as less politically threatening than their Army counterparts—and his reputation for moderation made him palatable to the genrō council.

=== National unity cabinet ===
Masuko formed a "national unity" cabinet that included members from both the major parties and the military establishment. The cabinet's stated purpose was to end the political crisis and restore the normal functioning of government. In practice, this meant acceding to the military's budget demands while maintaining the facade of civilian control.

The Masuko government's first major act was the passage of the Military Budget Expansion Act of 1913, 
[[File:Masuko Military Budget Expansion 1913.png|thumb|right|200px|The Military Budget Expansion Act of 1913 authorized significant increases in Army and Navy spending]]
which approved the increased spending that had triggered the political crisis. The act authorized significant increases in both Army and Navy budgets, with particular emphasis on naval construction. The Navy's share of the military budget increased from 35% to 42%, reflecting Masuko's own strategic priorities.

=== Shendan tensions ===
The Masuko government's most significant challenge was the growing tension with [[Illypnian]] colonial powers in the [[Shendan]] region. The Illypnian powers—particularly Volisania and Breisland—had been imposing increasingly restrictive trade regulations on Sanese commercial vessels operating in the Shendan, and there were growing calls within Okaiken for a more assertive response.

Masuko pursued a dual strategy of diplomatic negotiation and naval expansion. He authorized the construction of two new battlecruisers and four destroyers, significantly expanding the Navy's ability to project power into the Shendan. He also dispatched a naval squadron on a "goodwill tour" of Shendan ports—
[[File:Masuko Shendan Naval Demonstration 1914.png|thumb|right|200px|The naval "goodwill tour" of Shendan ports, widely interpreted as a warning to Illypnian colonial authorities]]
a demonstration of Sanese naval power that was widely interpreted as a warning to the Illypnian colonial authorities.

Domestically, Masuko's government maintained a cautious approach. He avoided confrontation with the political parties, allowing the Diet to proceed with its legislative agenda on non-military matters. The government passed the Commercial Shipping Subsidy Act of 1914, which provided financial support for Sanese shipping companies operating in the Shendan trade routes, and the Port Development Act of 1915, which funded the expansion of commercial port facilities.

The Masuko government also introduced the Income Tax Amendment of 1914, which raised the top marginal rate on high earners to fund the military expansion—a measure that was deeply unpopular with the business community but necessary to finance the naval construction program. The government also established the National Savings Promotion Bureau in 1915, which encouraged citizens to purchase government bonds to fund military spending. The Bureau's propaganda campaign, which linked patriotic duty to financial sacrifice, was a precursor to the more intensive civilian mobilization efforts of the Taisei Yokusankai era.

On social policy, Masuko's government was largely passive. The prime minister, a career naval officer with little interest in social reform, deferred to the Diet on domestic legislation. The Factory Act Amendment of 1915, which modestly expanded protections for women and child workers, was passed through Diet initiative rather than cabinet leadership.

=== Growing military influence ===
Despite his moderate intentions, Masuko's government was increasingly dominated by the military. The Army, emboldened by the success of its budget demands, began to assert its independence more aggressively. Army officers routinely bypassed the prime minister to communicate directly with the Emperor through the [[House of Officers of Okaiken|House of Officers]], a practice that undermined the principle of civilian control.

By 1916, Masuko's government had become little more than a vehicle for military policy. The prime minister himself recognized this, confiding to a close associate that "the cabinet has become a rubber stamp for the General Staff." When the Diet attempted to reduce the military budget in late 1916, the Army responded by demanding that Masuko dissolve the Diet—a demand that he refused.

Masuko resigned on 18 December 1916, having concluded that a military prime minister could not restore civilian control over the armed forces. He was succeeded by [[Kenzan Mihashi]], the first "commoner" prime minister, who was appointed as an independent reformer.

In the broader foreign policy context, the Masuko government also deepened Okaiken's involvement in [[Kwangju]] affairs. The government authorized increased economic investment in Kwangju infrastructure, including the construction of railways and the development of mining concessions, and expanded the military advisory mission to the Kwangju armed forces. These activities, while commercially beneficial, heightened tensions with [[Shansi]], which viewed growing Sanese influence in Kwangju as a threat to its own continental interests. The Masuko government's Mosellen policy was also significant: the government renewed the Mosellen-Sanese security pact of 1904, ensuring continued Mosellen diplomatic support for Sanese interests in the Shendan while avoiding any formal military alliance that might provoke the Illypnian colonial powers.

== Legacy ==
Reijiro Masuko is remembered as a well-intentioned but ultimately ineffective prime minister whose appointment demonstrated the impossibility of resolving the military-civilian conflict from within the system. His "national unity" government succeeded in restoring short-term stability, but it did so by acceding to the military's demands—a pattern that would be repeated by subsequent governments with increasingly disastrous consequences.

Masuko's naval expansion program had lasting significance, however. The battlecruisers and destroyers authorized during his premiership would form the core of the Sanese fleet for the next two decades, and his strategic vision of Sanese naval power in the Shendan would guide naval policy long after his resignation.

After his resignation, Masuko returned to the Navy and served on the Supreme War Council until his retirement in 1918. He died in [[Okami]] on 3 April 1919 at the age of 66.

